Specifically, if $u \in V_{even}$ and $v \in V_{odd}$, then $d(v)$ could be $d(u)+1$ or $d(u)-1$.
If $u, v \in V_{even}$, then $d(v) \le d(u)+1$ and $d(u) \le d(v)+1$. Since both are even, $|d(u)-d(v)|$ must be even, so it can be 0. But distances are distinct? No, distances are not necessarily distinct.
